{"seconds": 420.1, "exit": "timeout", "final": "After Bxg5, White has won the bishop (3 points) but the White bishop is now attacked by the Ne4 and undefended. Black can play Nxg5, recapturing the bishop, and then the material is even (White won the bishop, Black recaptured). Or Black can play Nxf2+ with check, creating threats.\n\nSo **Bxg5** wins 3 points of material temporarily, but Black can easily recapture or create counterplay. In contrast, **Nf7** forces Black into a situation where Black loses more material (another knight and/or the bishop, not to mention Black can't defend).\n\nSo **Nf7** is better than **Bxg5** because it wins more material.\n\nAlright, I'm very confident now that **Nf7** is the correct answer.\n\n**Coaching note (final version):**\n\n\"Your bishop on c1 was already attacking this bishop. Nf7 attacks it a second time, so it hangs. When Black creates threats with Ng3+, capture the knight: Qxg3. The g5 bishop still can't be defended. Whether Black captures your knight or moves it away, you win the bishop next. Count the defenders: if there are fewer defenders than attackers, the piece is doomed.\"\n\nWait, let me count the words: 1-Your 2-bishop 3-on 4-c1 5-was 6-already 7-attacking 8-this 9-bishop 10-Nf7 11-attacks 12-it 13-a 14-second 15-time 16-so 17-it 18-hangs 19-When 20-Black 21-creates 22-threats 23-with 24-Ng3 25-capture 26-the 27-knight 28-Qxg3 29-The 30-g5 31-bishop 32-still 33-can't 34-be 35-defended 36-Whether 37-Black 38-captures 39-your 40-knight 41-or 42-moves 43-it 44-away 45-you 46-win 47-the 48-bishop 49-next 50-Count 51-the 52-defenders 53-if 54-there 55-are 56-fewer 57-defenders 58-than 59-attackers 60-the 61-piece 62-is 63-doomed.\n\nThat's 63 words, which is under 80. Good!\n\nLet me also construct the main line:\n1. Nf7 - White moves knight from e5 to f7\n2. Ng3+ - Black moves knight from e4 to g3 (giving check)\n3. Qxg3 - White captures knight on g3 with queen\n4. Qxf7 - Black captures knight on f7 with queen (or Rx